A multiorbital tight-binding Hamiltonian was constructed from maximally localized Wannier functions using a basis of five Co $d$ orbitals and three Si $p$ orbitals per unit cell; the spin-dependent hopping integrals and onsite potentials were obtained from the first-principles bands and reproduce the ab initio band structure. This tight-binding model was used as the single-particle Hamiltonian in linear-response Kubo calculations of the dynamical conductivity $\sigma(\omega,T)$ with finite relaxation time $\tau$, where the conductivity is evaluated by
      $
      \sigma(\omega,T)=\frac{2i}{\omega}\frac{e^{2}}{\hbar}\sum_{m\le n}\int_{\mathrm{BZ}}\frac{d^{3}\boldsymbol{k}}{(2\pi)^{3}}
      \frac{|\langle n\boldsymbol{k}|\hat{v}_{\alpha}|m\boldsymbol{k}\rangle|^{2}}
      {\hbar\omega-(E_{n\boldsymbol{k}}-E_{m\boldsymbol{k}})+i\hbar/(2\tau)}
      \left(n_F(E_{n\boldsymbol{k}},T)-n_F(E_{m\boldsymbol{k}},T)\right),
      $
      with velocity operator $\hat{v}_{\alpha}=(1/i\hbar)[x_{\alpha},H]$ and impurity effects parametrized by $\tau$.
